    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d375">

                <pb facs="0518" n="498" sameAs="frus:frus1950v07/pg498"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">330/7–3050: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-07-30T16:00:00+03:00">July 30, 1950—4 p.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 30—12:39 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>243. In absence details Department’s thinking underlying contemplated resolution
                    for SC meeting August 2 (Depcirtel July 29, 3 a. m.),<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d375-f1"/> I have serious misgivings as to its desirability. Aside
                    from probability that USSR action at intervening meeting on August 1 will
                    introduce elements materially bearing on its aptness, it seems to me that SC
                    actions to date have established sound position acceptable to free world and
                    proposed resolution does not appear to augment basic platform on which we now
                    stand. From point of view of embarrassing Soviets further it seems to me effort
                    so transparent as to weaken chances its support by present friends.</p>
                <p>Also, I feel Soviets could put reverse English on last clause for purposes their
                    propaganda and make capital of our action in Indo-china, <pb facs="0519" n="499"
                        xml:id="pg499"/> Formosa, Philippines etc. as “action which might endanger
                    international peace and security”.</p>
                <p>Department pass USUN, London, Paris; repeated info USUN 33, London 77, Paris
                    67.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d375-f1" n="1" xml:id="d375-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it transmitted the text of the draft resolution contained in
                    telegram 78, July 29, 4 p. m., to New York, <ref target="#pg491">p.
                    491</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
